The '''Bugatti Divo''' is a [[mid-engine design|mid-engine]] track-focused [[sports car]] developed and manufactured by [[Bugatti Automobiles|Bugatti Automobiles S.A.S.]] The car is named after French racing driver [[Albert Divo]], who raced for Bugatti in the 1920s winning the [[Targa Florio]] race twice.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Westbrook |first=Justin T. |date=2018-08-24 |title=The 2019 Bugatti Divo Is the 236 MPH $5.8 Million Enthusiast's Bugatti |url=https://jalopnik.com/the-2019-bugatti-divo-is-the-236-mph-5-8-million-enthu-1828585730 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181019205655/https://jalopnik.com/the-2019-bugatti-divo-is-the-236-mph-5-8-million-enthu-1828585730 |archive-date=2018-10-19 |access-date=2018-10-19 |work=Jalopnik |language=en-US}}</ref> It was revealed on 24 August 2018 at “The Quail – A Motorsports Gathering“ in California, United States.<ref>{{cite press release |title="Happiness Is Not Around The Corner. It Is The Corner." |date=10 July 2018 |publisher=Bugatti |location=Molsheim |url=https://newsroom.bugatti.com/press-releases/happiness-is-not-around-the-corner-it-is-the-corner- |access-date=23 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221223040448/https://newsroom.bugatti.com/press-releases/happiness-is-not-around-the-corner-it-is-the-corner- |archive-date=23 December 2022}}</ref>

==Specifications and features==

[[File:GIMS 2019, Le Grand-Saconnex (GIMS0956).jpg|left|thumb|Interior]]

The car draws inspiration from the [[Bugatti Type 57|Bugatti Type 57SC Atlantic]] and the [[Bugatti Vision Gran Turismo]] concept in terms of design and has track performance as its main focus. The car includes a redesigned exhaust system featuring quad exhaust pipes, a 1.8 metre wide fixed rear wing (23% wider than the retractable wing on the [[Bugatti Chiron|Chiron]]), a [[NACA duct]] on the roof that channels air to the rear of the car on a central fin and ultimately on the rear wing for improved downforce, a large front chin spoiler, more refined side skirts, larger air intakes on the front, new head lamps and tail lamps, a vent in the bonnet for improved radiator cooling and vents on the front wheel arches to cool the brakes.

The interior in the Divo is relatively similar to the more luxurious Chiron, but has Alcantara upholstery and [[carbon-fibre]] trim in order to save weight.

Other notable changes include stiffer springs and dampers, carbon-fibre wiper blades and intercooler shroud, grooved wheel spokes, reduced sound insulation, a lighter sound system and removal of the storage lockers present in the doors and centre console for a weight saving of {{cvt|35|kg|lb|0|abbr=on}} over the Chiron Sport. The engine, a [[turbocharged petrol engines#quad-turbo|quad-turbocharged]] W16 unit, is retained from the Chiron along with the 7-speed [[dual-clutch transmission]].<ref name="TG">{{cite web |last=Rix |first=Jack |date=24 August 2018 |title=Meet the €5m, 236mph Bugatti Divo |url=https://www.topgear.com/car-news/pebble-beach-2018/meet-eu5m-236mph-bugatti-divo |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180826214629/https://www.topgear.com/car-news/pebble-beach-2018/meet-eu5m-236mph-bugatti-divo |archive-date=26 August 2018 |accessdate=26 August 2018 |website=TopGear |publisher=[[Top Gear (2002 TV series)|Top Gear]]}}</ref>

==Performance==

[[File:Bugatti Divo, GIMS 2019, Le Grand-Saconnex (GIMS9981).jpg|alt=|thumb|Rear view|left]]

The car is 8.0 seconds quicker than the Chiron around the [[Nardò Ring|Nardò]] test track according to the manufacturer and generates {{cvt|456|kg|lb|0|abbr=on}} of downforce at top speed, {{cvt|90|kg|lb|0|abbr=on}} more than the Chiron.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Stewart |first=Jack |date=2018-08-24 |title=Bugatti One-Ups Itself With Its New Divo Supercar |url=https://www.wired.com/story/bugatti-new-divo-supercar/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181020013036/https://www.wired.com/story/bugatti-new-divo-supercar/ |archive-date=2018-10-20 |access-date=2018-10-19 |magazine=WIRED |language=en-US}}</ref> The top speed is, however, reduced to {{cvt|236|mph|kph|0|abbr=on|order=flip}}, owing to the extra drag produced by the aerodynamic elements and due to excessive pressure on the tyres resulting from a lower ride height. Power output is unchanged from the Chiron, with {{Convert|1103|kW|PS hp|0|abbr=on}} at 6,700 rpm and {{convert|1600|Nm|lbft|0|abbr=on}} of torque at from 2,000 to 6,000 rpm. Acceleration from {{convert|0–100|km/h|mph|0|abbr=on}} is also unchanged from the Chiron at 2.4 seconds but the Divo has a higher lateral acceleration (1.6 g vs 1.5 g in the Chiron).<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.bugatti.com/divo/|title=Official Bugatti Divo: Built for corners!|website=www.bugatti.com|language=en|access-date=2018-10-19|archive-date=2021-09-22 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210922023321/https://www.bugatti.com/divo/|url-status=dead}}</ref> The Divo lacks the top speed mode unlocked with a special key as present on the Chiron.

==Production== 
The production of the Divo is limited to 40 units and the car will be built alongside the Chiron at the Bugatti factory. All of the 40 cars were pre-sold before the public debut to Chiron owners through special invitations from dealers.<ref name="TG"/> The Bugatti Divo sold out in its first day of availability.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Im |first=Jimmy |date=2018-09-10 |title=This new $5.8 million Bugatti hypercar sold out in 1 day — check it out |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2018/09/10/photos-bugatti-divo-hypercar-sold-out-in-one-day.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180910195115/https://www.cnbc.com/2018/09/10/photos-bugatti-divo-hypercar-sold-out-in-one-day.html |archive-date=2018-09-10 |access-date=2018-09-11 |work=CNBC}}</ref> In April 2020, Divo entered its final phase of testing with deliveries beginning later in the year.<ref name="Autodevot">{{Cite web |last=Yap |first=Chips |date=2020-04-28 |title=Bugatti Divo hypercar reaches final phase of development |url=https://www.piston.my/2020/04/28/bugatti-divo-hypercar-reaches-final-phase-of-development/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200923164746/https://www.piston.my/2020/04/28/bugatti-divo-hypercar-reaches-final-phase-of-development/ |archive-date=2020-09-23 |access-date=2026-01-03 |website=Piston.my |language=en-US}}</ref> The first car was delivered in August 2020.<ref>{{cite press release |title=Bugatti Divo – Delivery to Customers Begins |date=5 August 2020 |publisher=Bugatti |location=Molsheim |url=https://newsroom.bugatti.com/press-releases/bugatti-divo-deliveries-to-customers-begins |access-date=23 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221223082544/https://newsroom.bugatti.com/press-releases/bugatti-divo-deliveries-to-customers-begins |archive-date=23 December 2022}}</ref> On July 23, 2021, the last Bugatti Divo was produced.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Smith|first=Christopher|date=July 22, 2021|title=Last Bugatti Divo Leaves Molsheim Looking As Stunning As Ever|url=https://www.motor1.com/news/521913/last-bugatti-divo-production-car/|url-status=live|access-date=July 23, 2021|website=Motor1|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210722173611/https://www.motor1.com/news/521913/last-bugatti-divo-production-car/ |archive-date=2021-07-22 }}</ref>

